                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Solves A x = b when there are more equations than unknowns and no x satisfies them all exactly - the usual case with measured data. It finds the x that misses by the least: the smallest sum of squared differences between A x and b.\n\nFitting a straight line through noisy points is the classic case: each point says slope x its position + offset = its value; with many points the line that best agrees with all of them is the answer. Fitting a calibration, a trend, or how much of each known pattern a reading contains all take the same form.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.linalg.lstsq\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nA matrix:\nOne row per equation (per measurement), one column per unknown. Every input solves and sends - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nb:\nThe measured values, one per row of A, held. A matrix fits several sets at once.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nx:\nThe best-fitting unknowns.\n\nresiduals:\nThe sum of the squared misses that remain: 0 for a perfect fit. Sent first.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there. On the Mac's GPU (mps), torch has no lstsq: it is done on the CPU and the answer moved back.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nSix points near the line 2 x position + 1, four of them 0.25 off. Each row of A is a position and a 1 (for the offset). The misses balance out, so the fit finds slope 2 and offset 1 exactly; the residual, 0.25, is the four misses of 0.25, squared and added.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.lstsq is the numpy version. scipy.linregress fits a line and reports how good it is. scipy.curve_fit fits any formula. t.linalg.solve is the exact case."
        },
